/*! @arch_page arch-disagg-step-down Step-Down

Step-down transfers shared-write responsibility without restarting the old leader and changes
it to a follower. To do this, the application needs a clear boundary between incoming writes
that belong in the shared checkpoint handed to the next leader and later writes that must be
stored locally for follower operations.

@section disagg-step-down-motivation Blocking step-down

The simplest approach, once the application decides to step down, is to take a concluding
checkpoint and then call WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with
<code>disaggregated=(role=follower)</code>. This approach cannot accept writes that will not be
included in the concluding checkpoint. Until reconfiguration, the node still operates as a
leader and writes to its live stable tables. Reconfiguration switches those tables to read-only
checkpoint views, so updates newer than the checkpoint would no longer be available through
the follower's stable tables.

Using only checkpoint and reconfiguration therefore requires the application to stop new
writes and finish all write transactions before checkpointing. It advances the stable timestamp
to cover their changes, takes the concluding checkpoint, and then reconfigures the connection
as a follower, keeping writes stopped throughout these actions. This is <b>blocking step-down</b>:
the concluding checkpoint's latency becomes part of the period in which the application cannot
write.

@section disagg-step-down-preparation Asynchronous step-down

Asynchronous step-down allows writes to continue while the concluding checkpoint is taken by
defining the boundary in advance. The application sets a step-down timestamp N. Write
transactions beginning after it is set also replicate their writes to the local ingest tables and
must commit at timestamps above N. The concluding checkpoint is taken at N. The node can then
switch to checkpointed stable table views without losing its newer writes, which remain in the ingest tables.

This separates taking the concluding checkpoint from the step-down reconfiguration. Write
transactions that have not finished when the step-down timestamp is set are rolled back. Writes are
not allowed during the step-down reconfiguration, so asynchronous step-down only shortens the period
of write unavailability but does not eliminate it.

The application is responsible for replicating or replaying operations above N on the new
leader. They are not part of the concluding checkpoint, and retaining them in the old
leader's ingest tables does not transfer them to another node. Applying these operations on the
new leader allows it to include their changes in subsequent shared checkpoints.

@section disagg-step-down-lifecycle Asynchronous lifecycle

The usual flow of asynchronous step-down is:

1. <b>Set the step-down timestamp:</b> choose N newer than the commit timestamps of all
   transactions already committed, subject to the timestamp API contract below, and call
   WT_CONNECTION::set_timestamp with <code>step_down_timestamp=N</code>.
2. <b>Create the concluding checkpoint:</b> advance <code>stable_timestamp</code> to N once all
   transactions with commit timestamps at or below N have committed or rolled back, and call
   WT_SESSION::checkpoint.
3. <b>Stop serving write operations:</b> once the checkpoint is complete, stop new writes and
   wait for all ongoing write transactions to finish. Writes must remain stopped during
   the step-down reconfiguration.
4. <b>Reconfigure:</b> call WT_CONNECTION::reconfigure with
   <code>disaggregated=(role=follower)</code> to switch WiredTiger to follower operations.

The step-down timestamp must remain unchanged until the step-down reconfiguration clears it.
The stable timestamp must not advance above N during the step-down process. Both the stable
timestamp and the last completed shared checkpoint timestamp must equal N at reconfiguration.

@section disagg-step-down-requirements Application requirements

The application must meet the following requirements and the timestamp API contract described
in WT_CONNECTION::set_timestamp:

- The step-down timestamp must be set only once per step-down/reconfiguration cycle, on a leader.
- N must be higher than every committed transaction's commit timestamp when it is set.
- Write transactions beginning after N is set must commit with a timestamp higher than N.
- Layered-table reads and writes must use explicit snapshot-isolation transactions. Auto-commit,
  read-committed, and read-uncommitted transactions must not occur during the step-down process.
- WT_SESSION::reset_snapshot operations must not occur during the step-down process.
- All write transactions must finish before the step-down reconfiguration. No writes or
  active write transactions may run during that call.
- Prepared transactions and truncate operations must finish before the step-down timestamp is
  set and must not occur during the step-down process.
- Operations above N must be replicated or replayed on the new leader.

When schema epochs are used, the application must also meet the requirements in
@ref disagg-step-down-schema.

@section disagg-step-down-transactions Why straddling writers are rolled back

A transaction's commit timestamp may be set after some of its writes have already occurred.
WiredTiger therefore chooses the write destination (ingest table or stable table) using the
step-down state recorded at transaction begin, rather than a commit timestamp that may not yet
be known.

A writer that began before the step-down timestamp was set may already have written to the
stable tables. If it committed above N, its updates would not be included in the concluding
checkpoint and would have no ingest table copy to survive the step-down reconfiguration,
leading to data loss.
WiredTiger rolls back such a writer when a subsequent write or commit check observes that the
step-down timestamp has been set. The application retries the whole transaction, which now
begins with N set and writes to the ingest tables.

@section disagg-step-down-schema Schema changes during the step-down

Data timestamps and <b>schema epochs</b> describe different boundaries: one selects record
versions, while the other selects schema operations included in shared metadata. Creating or
dropping a table locally does not itself publish the change to other nodes. The application
replicates schema operations, and shared checkpoints carry the corresponding metadata. With
schema epochs, WT_SESSION::publish assigns epochs to pending schema operations for a table;
checkpointing includes published operations through the stable schema epoch.

When schema epochs are in use, <code>step_down_disaggregated_schema_epoch=E</code> must be
provided together with the step-down timestamp N. Before taking the concluding checkpoint,
the schema operations required through epoch E must be completed and published, and
<code>stable_disaggregated_schema_epoch</code> must advance to E. E must not be older than the
existing stable schema epoch. The stable schema epoch must not advance above E during the
step-down process and must remain at E through the step-down reconfiguration.

A layered table created after the step-down timestamp is set has the follower table shape:
only its local ingest table is created. It has no stable table to include in the concluding
checkpoint, and its writes remain ingest-only. A later step-up creates the missing stable
table. The application must replicate or replay schema operations after the boundary on the
new leader as well as their associated data writes.

When publishing during step-down, the table's latest create or drop determines the permitted
epoch: above E if it occurred after the step-down timestamp was set, and at or below E if it
occurred before. Publication must also be above the current stable schema epoch.

@section disagg-step-down-write-conflict Write conflict detection

After setting the step-down timestamp, writes from new transactions must be retained in the
ingest tables. The default mode also writes them to the stable tables, so earlier and later
writers use the default btree's conflict detection. The legacy mode writes only to the ingest
tables and checks for conflicts across the two constituents:

- <b>Mirrored writes (default):</b> with <code>disaggregated=(stepdown_write_mirroring=true)</code>,
  writes from transactions beginning after the step-down timestamp is set on existing layered
  tables go to the stable table first and then to the ingest table. Since all write transactions
  on these tables, including those beginning after the step-down timestamp is set, write to the
  stable table, WiredTiger's normal single-btree conflict detection handles conflicts between
  them and provides consistent behavior. Both copies belong to the same transaction.
- <b>Ingest-only writes (legacy):</b> with <code>disaggregated=(stepdown_write_mirroring=false)</code>,
  write transactions beginning after the step-down timestamp is set write to the ingest tables
  only. Each ingest-only cursor write performs an explicit check against the stable table for
  conflicting updates. This avoids writing an additional copy to the stable table, but requires
  cross-table conflict detection. It does not always detect
  conflicts immediately with writes from straddling transactions that will be rolled back.
  These missed conflicts do not cause persistent data corruption because the straddling
  transactions involved are rolled back rather than committed.

Mirroring adds stable table writes and extra reconciliation overhead, but retains the ingest
table copy needed after the step-down reconfiguration. Its stable table versions above N remain
excluded from the concluding checkpoint. Tables created after the step-down timestamp is set
have no stable table and remain ingest-only in either mode.

*/
